Let \[f(n) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
n^2-2 & \text{ if }n<0, \\
2n-20 & \text{ if }n \geq 0.
\end{array}
\right.\]What is the positive difference between the two values of $a$ that satisfy the equation $f(-2)+f(2)+f(a)=0$?

solution_steps:
From the given information, we have the equations:
\begin{align*}
a - 7 &= N \\
b + 7 &= N \\
c \cdot 7 &= N
\end{align*}
We can solve these equations to find the values of $a, b,$ and $c$ in terms of $N$:
\begin{align*}
a &= N + 7 \\
b &= N - 7 \\
c &= \frac{N}{7}
\end{align*}
Substituting these values into the equation $a + b + c = 60$, we get:
\begin{align*}
(N + 7) + (N - 7) + \frac{N}{7} &= 60 \\
2N + \frac{N}{7} &= 60 \\
\frac{15N}{7} &= 60 \\
15N &= 420 \\
N &= \boxed{28}
\end{align*}
The value of $N$ is 28.The answer is: 28

