solution_steps:
We substitute $x = 3$ into the expression $x^3 + x^2 + x + 1$:
\begin{align*}
(3)^3 + (3)^2 + (3) + 1 &= 27 + 9 + 3 + 1 \\
&= 40.
\end{align*}
Therefore, when $x = 3$, the value of $x^3 + x^2 + x + 1$ is $\boxed{40}$.The answer is: 40

We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 39 and 91.
\[\begin{array}{r|rr}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{91} & \\
\cline{2-3}
39 & 2 & \text{(quotient)} \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{13} & \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & \text{(remainder)}
\end{array}\]
Since the remainder is 0, we have found that the GCD of 39 and 91 is $\boxed{13}$.
The answer is: 13

